    "body": "<p>This Signature model designs a rainforest dawn as the densest virtual chorus in the suite. Sixty virtual species are assigned bands, time slots, and call rates, rain is scheduled as a geophony interruption, and the model reports total occupied bandwidth, the overlap that forces time-sharing, and the chorus&#x27;s recovery time after the rain stops. The design rule is the acoustic niche rule: bands first, time slots second, and the model scores how well a given assignment follows it. The rainforest is invented, the species are virtual, and any rendered dawn sound is labelled SIMULATED. Its purpose is teaching dense-soundscape organisation at its most demanding and giving composers a rainforest template.</p><h4>Science proof</h4><p>Science proof: the model encodes the documented rainforest pattern: extreme species richness, band and time partitioning, rain interruptions, and rapid chorus recovery. Those patterns are published rainforest soundscape findings, and the model reproduces them in simplified, parameter-explicit form.</p><h4>Math proof</h4><p>Math proof: if 60 virtual species each occupy an average band of 300 hertz, total occupied bandwidth is 60 x 300 = 18000 hertz, which exceeds a 12000-hertz model band by 18000 - 12000 = 6000 hertz, so time-sharing is required. The demo prints total occupied 18000 Hz, model band 12000 Hz, and excess 6000 Hz.</p><h4>Soundscape layer(s)</h4><p>biophony; geophony</p><h4>Source</h4><p>Signature design study for this database, October 2026; rainforest soundscape structure as in the public edition</p>",
